1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gards- og ættesoge for Sveio I page 161 Haugland (29f).
2 CONT
2 CONT To America 1856.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/153206863/nels-h-nelson
2 CONT
2 CONT Nels H. Nelson, a prosperous farmer of the Town of Curran, died at his home there on October 9th after a lingering illness of asthma and heart trouble, at the age of 65 years, 2 months and 18 days.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Nelson was born at Bergensift, Norway , on July 23, 1850. He came to America in 1856. In 1872 he was united in marriage at Beaver Creek to Miss Christine Brown, and shortly after their marriage they moved to the Town of Curran
2 CONC , where they have resided for forty-three years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by his widow and six children:
2 CONT 1. Mrs. Thea Syvertson and
2 CONT 2. Mrs. Louisa Pearson, of McVille, North Dakota;
2 CONT 3. Nicholi Nelson of Devils Lake, North Dakota;
2 CONT 4. Mrs. Josephine Johnson of Newfolden, Minnesota;
2 CONT 5. Miss Minnie Nelson of Currran
2 CONT and an adopted child,
2 CONT 6. Mrs. Chistine Knudston of Bloomer.
2 CONT Two children died a number of years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also leaves to mourn his departure one sister, Mrs. Rier Thompson of Beach, North Dakota. Her name was Bromhilde Serine Haugland. She is FAG Memorial # 18953041
2 CONT
2 CONT Two weeks ago Mr. Nelson was taken seriously ill, but seemed to be on a gain until Saturday when quietly and peacefully the spirit took its flight to the other shore. A host of friends and neighbors mourn the loss of this kind, ac
2 CONC tive man and showed their esteem for him by a large attendance and a wreath of flowers at his funeral. He leaves a fond memory in the hearts of his wife and children, as he was always a loving husband and father.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral services were conducted by Rev. Borgen of Taylor at the home and at the Curran Valley Lutheran Church of which he was a faithful member. His whole life was that of a true Christian man, responding to the call of duty i
2 CONC n an unostentatious manner, and performing his part with strict fidelity. In his home he was a devoted husband and father. In his community he was a kind and obliging neighbor, ever willing to lend a helping hand. In business tran
2 CONC sactions he was prompt and careful in meeting every obligation and responsibility. In every phase of his life he displayed that sterling character, strict integrity and loyalty to right and justice which marked him as a man among 
2 CONC men who could be depended upon in every emerging. It is the character of such men which puts the stamp of honesty and integrity upon a community. It is the example of such men which marks the pathway for youth and serves as a guid
2 CONC e for those of mature years. The influence of such men is unending. Their good lives live after them.
2 CONT
2 CONT BADGER STATE BANNER - OCTOBER 20, 1915
